16268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116399 #edb/ref 1061 9160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116399 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25c91feb-798e-5857-9090-3b36088bc71b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116399 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116449 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ shown below. \n\n![](images/6f/6fdb9a8337d16f1ef1910989b3df92844c5393c33108f98ae7993cc939bed0b3.png)\n\nNow, let's draw a line segment that \n\n- runs from $\\color{blue}C$ to the line ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}},$ and\n\n- is *perpendicular* to ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}}.$\n\nThis line segment is the dashed segment shown below:\n\n![](images/4a/4a7ee2771c9b0c60dbb07c43b0439e822ea9b2c261ac41af9b44fec72b9f2a91.png)\n\nFinally, let's label the other endpoint of our segment ${\\color{blue}{H}}.$\n\n![](images/75/758eab533719220515460b6677054438a9ac0e2701d1a5267b973e526f63bd90.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The line segment ${\\color{blue}\\overline{CH}}$ is called the **height** (or **altitude**) *corresponding to* ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}}.$\n\n- The line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ is called the **base** of the altitude ${\\color{blue}\\overline{CH}}.$\n\nAs we'll soon discover, the heights and their corresponding bases are important attributes of triangles." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116449 #edb/ref 1065 2232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116449 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bdb9bfbd-b830-51f0-8712-5d34f83f2812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116449 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116450 #edb/ref 1064 "We can draw exactly three heights (or altitudes) in any triangle. However, sometimes the height lies outside the triangle.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$ below.\n\n![](images/df/df1ac2c1074305f8ead506d9aeb8c3927ad51ae5949b45976f1971b52d402ee5.png)\n\nThe height corresponding to $\\overline{BC}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $A$ to the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$\n\nIn this case, it's impossible to draw a perpendicular from $A$ to the *segment* $\\overline{BC}.$ However, we can first *extend* the line segment $\\overline{BC}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26bf9eb8af1e9b71c263c08cae5797d5c911410985cdc01213ac2c295f7b8d46.png)\n\nNow, we draw the perpendicular from $A$ to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$ This gives the height $\\overline{AH}{:}$\n\n![](images/89/894686fce1393ebcf998be6885020627c9f3cde5916adfec67234f15f8895826.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116450 #edb/ref 1065 2236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116450 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4031d801-f4b8-543f-ad4d-9ef60d513427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116450 #edb/ref 1067 "Case of Extended Bases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293090 #edb/ref 1080 "{30}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293090 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293090 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "250975f6-1855-51d4-855b-4756eab86379" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293411 #edb/ref 1080 "{18}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293411 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293411 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74682fc2-217c-57f5-ab98-c931310e5466" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293433 #edb/ref 1080 "{15}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293433 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293433 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7d0ac39b-3059-5c95-9035-fede9601ced2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293712 #edb/ref 1080 "{20}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293712 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293712 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8f82474-ac34-5dcf-9935-8a82efc05435" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293825 #edb/ref 1080 "{25}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293825 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293825 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e390ae5f-9602-5bc0-bd7b-333ec91cd6ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186293090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186293411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186293433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186293712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186293825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294264 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294264 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294264 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186293712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294264 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5dff0f5f-38be-5491-8c48-4be6dc5f48b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294980 #edb/ref 1069 "q-27832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294980 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294980 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/66/6665784a8ce048fd26fbd97038346e8e6fdf6800d645ea0488f1c18986f5864b.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $△ABC$ shown above. The line segment $\\overset{―}{CH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ If $m∠BAC={110}^{∘},$ calculate $m∠HCA.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294980 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94753d7f-95e4-5b39-9c21-ede7ecea7547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294980 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HAC+m∠BAC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HAC+{110}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HAC & ={70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $△ACH$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HCA+m∠HAC+m∠AHC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA+{70}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA+{160}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
